  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bullpen imploded in one inning and the lead never came back.",
    "what_this_means": "Eight runs in the eighth. That was the game. Jacob Lopez gave them six solid innings. Two runs on five hits, five strikeouts. And the offense scratched out enough to lead through seven. Zack Gelof drove in two with a home run. McNeil added a hit. Then the pen collapsed. Seth Johnson couldn't get anyone out. Jos\u00e9 Suarez gave up four more before he could record an out. Scott Blewett cleaned up the mess but the damage was done. The Athletics left runners on base three times in the first eight innings and converted one of those chances into actual runs. That margin. Six to four. Held until the eighth inning decided it wasn't enough.",
    "what_to_watch": "The problem is not inconsistency anymore. It is vulnerability. They can pitch into the seventh and stay ahead. They can get enough hits to matter. But one bad inning from the wrong relievers erases all of that. Two runs in three innings from a fresh bullpen says the structure is there. Eight runs from the same bullpen says it is not. This is what a 47-72 team looks like when it almost catches up. Close enough to see it, not close enough to hold it. The eighth inning is where stretches get longer.",
